<TopicPage slug="subjects/law/intermediate/criminal-vs-civil-law">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Every legal dispute sits on one of two tracks. <strong>Criminal law</strong> punishes offences
    against society — the state prosecutes, and liberty is at stake, so proof must be near
    certain. <strong>Civil law</strong> settles disputes between private parties — money and
    obligations are at stake, so "more likely than not" suffices. Same event, different courts,
    different standards, different consequences. Once you see the two tracks as answers to two
    different questions — <em>what does the community do to wrongdoers?</em> and <em>who owes
    what to whom?</em> — every structural difference between them follows logically.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four structural differences — with the famous case that shows them all" widget="equationStep" config={ccCfg} />

  <h2>Two tracks, two purposes, two casts of characters</h2>
  <p>
    The deepest difference is <em>purpose</em>. A crime is a wrong against the community: the
    rule says "don't do this", the prosecution enforces it on everyone's behalf, and the
    sanction is <strong>punishment</strong> — expressing blame, deterring others, protecting the
    public, (in theory) rehabilitating. A civil wrong is a breach of duty owed to a
    particular person, and the remedy is <strong>correction</strong> — restoring the injured party,
    so far as money can, to where they would have been. Hence the vocabulary. Criminal cases
    are brought by the state — in England and Wales the Crown Prosecution Service (created
    1985), which took charging decisions away from the police — and are styled
    <em>R v Defendant</em> (Rex or Regina, the Crown). Notice the victim's role: a victim does
    not "press charges", cannot withdraw a prosecution, and may see the case proceed even
    after forgiving the offender. Civil cases are styled <em>Claimant v Defendant</em> (US:
    plaintiff), and the claimant is master of the case — settling, abandoning or escalating
    it at will.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> people say "he got off" when a defendant is acquitted.
    An acquittal means proof failed the criminal standard — it does not mean innocence was
    proven, and it forecloses nothing on the civil track, where a different standard applies.
  </p>

  <h2>The standards of proof: the quantified heart of the distinction</h2>
  <p>
    The criminal standard — <strong>beyond reasonable doubt</strong> (English judges tell juries
    "sure" or "so sure you are certain"; US juries hear "proof beyond a reasonable doubt") —
    exists because the criminal system's errors are catastrophic: convicting the innocent
    destroys a life and corrupts the system's legitimacy. Blackstone's ratio (1769) is the
    classic statement: "better that ten guilty persons escape than that one innocent
    suffer". The civil standard — the <strong>balance of probabilities</strong> (US:
    preponderance of the evidence) — just asks which story is more likely than not, above
    50 per cent, because ordering someone to pay compensation is a survivable error.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>O.J. Simpson</em> is the case that makes the whole structure visible. In October 1995 a
    criminal jury acquitted him of two murders; in February 1997 a civil jury found him
    liable for the deaths and awarded $33.5 million (including $25 million punitive damages)
    to the families. Contradiction? No mechanism allows it: the criminal jury needed proof
    beyond reasonable doubt; the civil jury needed "more likely than not", saw the same
    evidence, and could lawfully reach the opposite bottom line. The case is also a lesson in
    why the double standard exists at all — the criminal bar is deliberately set where it is
    hard to clear, and everything else in this page follows from that tilt.
  </p>

  <h2>What each track requires of you: the elements of liability</h2>
  <p>
    On the criminal side, a prosecutor must generally prove two elements. <strong>Actus
    reus</strong> — the guilty act: a voluntary act (or a legally culpable omission) causing the
    forbidden result. <strong>Mens rea</strong> — the guilty mind: intention, recklessness or
    negligence as the offence demands. Both are needed because neither alone is fair: the
    driver who has an unpredictable epileptic seizure did not choose the act; the person who
    plans a crime never committed has not done it. A handful of regulatory offences —
    speeding, many health-and-safety breaches — impose <strong>strict liability</strong>, skipping
    mens rea because enforcement would otherwise be impossible and the stakes are low
    enough. Defences then attack an element or excuse it: insanity, duress, self-defence,
    intoxication, consent — each a rule about when society forfeits its right to blame.
  </p>
  <p>
    The civil side's core is <strong>negligence</strong>, with four elements: a <em>duty of
    care</em>, a <em>breach</em> of the standard of the reasonable person, <em>causation</em>, and
    <em>loss</em>. Other torts — defamation (protecting reputation), nuisance (protecting use
    and enjoyment of land), trespass — follow the same pattern: a protected interest, a duty,
    a breach, damage. Notice the polarity shift: criminal law asks "what kind of person are
    you?" (blame); civil law asks "who should bear this loss?" (allocation). That is why
    contributory negligence splits the loss between the parties in civil law, while
    "the victim was careless" is not a defence to assault.
  </p>

  <h2>Remedies: punishment versus restoration</h2>
  <p>
    Criminal remedies are calibrated to blame: imprisonment (indeterminate for the most
    serious), fines payable <em>to the state</em>, community orders, confiscation of
    proceeds. Civil remedies are calibrated to the claimant's loss: <strong>compensatory
    damages</strong> (putting the claimant where they would have been — lost earnings, medical
    costs, pain and suffering), <strong>aggravated</strong> damages where the conduct was
    humiliating, and rare <strong>exemplary</strong> (punitive) damages where a defendant
    profited from wrongdoing; plus non-money remedies — an <strong>injunction</strong>
    ordering or forbidding conduct (the famous restraint of newspapers before publication),
    or <strong>specific performance</strong>, compelling performance of a contract, granted
    where money cannot fix the harm (a unique house, a rare painting). The asymmetry tells
    you which track you are on: if the money goes to the government, it is criminal; if it
    goes to the person wronged, it is civil.
  </p>

  <h2>The courts and the lay people who run them</h2>
  <p>
    Each track has its own architecture. Criminal cases start in the
    <strong>magistrates' courts</strong>, which handle roughly 95 per cent of criminal cases in
    England and Wales — and whose magistrates are unpaid, part-time volunteers (Justices of
    the Peace) advised on law by a legal adviser: laypeople judging their neighbours, a
    medieval institution still doing the bulk of the work. The more serious
    either-way and indictable offences go to the <strong>Crown Court</strong>, where a jury of
    twelve randomly selected citizens decides guilt (conviction requires unanimity or a
    10–2 majority, under the Juries Act 1974) while the judge rules on law. The civil track
    runs through the <strong>County Court</strong> and the <strong>High Court</strong> — almost always
    judge alone, no juries — before appeals to the Court of Appeal and Supreme Court. And
    because civil trials are slow and ruinously expensive (with costs that can dwarf the
    claim), the system actively routes disputes away from them: <strong>mediation</strong>,
    <strong>arbitration</strong> (binding, private, the world's international commerce runs on
    it), and <strong>ombudsmen</strong> for consumers. Well under 5 per cent of issued civil
    claims ever reach trial; settlement is the system's normal output, not its failure.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the line matters: morality, blame and the Hart–Devlin debate</h2>
  <p>
    The line between the tracks is also the line between law and morality, and it moves.
    Criminal law does something civil law never does: it says <em>this is shameful</em> —
    conviction is public condemnation in the community's name. That raises the question Lord
    Devlin and H.L.A. Hart fought over after the 1957 Wolfenden report (which recommended
    decriminalising homosexual acts): may the state criminalise private conduct that most
    citizens find immoral? Devlin said yes — shared morality is the "cement" of society;
    Hart said no — harm to others is the proper limit, and morality enforced by law is
    neither free nor truly moral. Hart's harm principle broadly won the argument in practice
    (consensual private conduct has been steadily decriminalised), but the debate keeps
    returning with each new question: drug use, assisted dying, online speech. Watch any
    such argument and you are watching the criminal–civil line being negotiated: society
    deciding whether it answers with condemnation or leaves the matter to private
    adjustment.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the tracks meet: one event, two cases</h2>
  <p>
    Real events routinely spawn both. A pub fight produces a prosecution for assault
    (Crown Court, beyond reasonable doubt, possible prison) <em>and</em> a claim by the injured
    man for damages (County Court, balance of probabilities, payable by — very often — the
    defendant's liability insurer, who was invisible in the criminal case). A fatal crash
    brings a prosecution for causing death by dangerous driving and a dependency claim under
    the Fatal Accidents Act 1976. The sequencing is informally but firmly "criminal first":
    criminal courts can take longer, and their findings (though not acquittals) often
    feed the civil case, while civil disclosure can complicate a fair trial. Parallel
    tracks on one set of facts is the system's normal condition, not a curiosity.
  </p>

  <h2>Double jeopardy and its exceptions</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Double jeopardy</strong> — the bar on trying someone twice for the same offence —
    protects finality: the state has had its full attempt, and an acquitted person must be
    able to live. But the protection has two boundaries. First, a civil claim is a different
    jeopardy entirely — which is the Simpson lesson. Second, in England and Wales the rule
    itself was softened: the Criminal Justice Act 2003 allows the Court of Appeal to quash an
    acquittal for a list of serious offences where there is <em>new and compelling evidence</em>
    and the DPP consents to a retrial. Its first use: the 2012 conviction of Gary Dobson and
    David Norris for the racist murder of Stephen Lawrence (1993), after new forensic
    evidence emerged — a change justified as fixing one injustice without reopening the
    floodgates, though critics still argue finality is worth its price.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Access to justice is the first expert caveat. The two tracks are only as equal as the
    wallets behind them: civil litigation costs (often tens of thousands of pounds even for
    modest claims, after the legal-aid cuts of the LASPO Act 2012) mean the settlement
    pressure described above is frequently <em>financial exhaustion</em> rather than informed
    choice, and criminal defendants without legal representation now routinely appear alone
    in the magistrates' courts. The standards of proof are formal; the resources to reach
    them are not evenly distributed — which is why "access to justice" is a syllabus topic
    in its own right.
  </p>
  <p>
    The two tracks blur at the edges, and the blurs are instructive. <strong>Contempt of
    court</strong> is quasi-criminal punishment in civil clothing. <strong>Regulatory
    enforcement</strong> (the FCA fining banks, environmental agencies prosecuting polluters)
    deliberately mixes the tracks: strict liability, civil penalties, criminal backstop.
    <strong>Restorative justice</strong> — offender meets victim, apologises, repays — asks
    whether punishment's expressive job could be done with restitution's methods, and the
    trial evidence (reduced reoffending in some randomised studies) keeps it alive as more
    than sentiment. Systems differ structurally too: continental <strong>inquisitorial</strong>
    procedure has an investigating judge build the file, against the Anglo-American
    <strong>adversarial</strong> contest — yet both preserve the same two-track structure and the
    same proof asymmetry, which suggests the distinction is not a national artefact but a
    feature of law itself. The live open questions: whether disclosure failures (as in the
    collapsed UK rape prosecutions of 2017–18) erode the criminal standard in practice; and
    whether litigation costs have made the civil track a luxury good, pushing ordinary
    disputes into settlement by financial exhaustion rather than informed choice.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> standard first-year criminal and civil procedure texts; Glanville Williams, Learning the Law; Blackstone, Commentaries (1769); Criminal Justice Act 2003; Juries Act 1974.</p>
